COMPOSITION RULE, HIGHEST PRIORITY: the scene fills the entire 16:9 frame edge to edge as a single full-bleed image — never a letterbox band, never an empty panel, never a flat colour block. The bottom three-eighths of the image height — that is the lowest 37%, roughly the bottom 270 pixels of a 720-pixel-tall frame — is the same continuous scene rendered dark, smoky and free of detail so overlay text stays readable, while all faces, flags, weapons and key objects sit entirely within the upper five-eighths. Anything held or carried sits at mid height, never near the bottom edge. Style: heavy digital oil painting worked over a photographic base, thick visible brushwork and impasto texture on flags, smoke and fabric, dramatic hard lighting, heavy atmospheric haze, one dominant colour drowning the frame, every part of the upper five-eighths packed edge to edge with elements and no empty space, cinematic wide-angle, very high contrast, painterly and rough rather than clean — a matte-painting finish, NOT a crisp photo composite, NOT a studio cut-out. Scene: the camera stands on the broken deck of a concrete road bridge at the height of the roadway and looks straight along its axis, so the severed span opens as a black gap directly ahead with twisted reinforcement bars clawing out of the torn concrete and shallow lagoon water glinting far below. An enormous Russian tricolour — horizontal white, blue and red bands — fills the entire left half of the frame from top to bottom, hung from a wrecked gantry, its heavy fabric painted with thick visible brushwork, edges charred black and torn into ragged strips, burn holes eaten through the cloth, soot staining the folds, the colours desaturated and grimy, flame eating slowly along one edge. Standing right in front of the flag at the left, chest-up and taking a third of the frame width, is a Russian transport-corps driver in his thirties in a dust-caked uniform: one hand clamped hard over his own mouth at mid height, eyes wide and locked on the gap, eyebrows lifted high, face drained pale but thrown bright by an orange key light from the fires so he separates cleanly from the dark. Behind him a line of military supply trucks is halted nose to tail on the intact half of the bridge, headlights smeared by haze. A column of fire and black smoke rises through the middle of the frame and cuts it vertically; the sky above is a black smoke-choked ceiling with drifting embers. The lower part of the picture continues into unlit water and oily smoke with no detail. no text, no letters, no words, no numbers, no captions, no watermark.

